      "content": "To understand how the measurement collapses the wave function, let's dive deeper into the mathematical framework of quantum mechanics.\n\nIn quantum mechanics, the wave function is a mathematical object that encodes the probability of finding a particle in different states. When two particles are entangled, their wave functions are combined into a single, entangled wave function. This wave function is a superposition of all possible states for both particles.\n\nWhen a measurement is made on one particle, the wave function of that particle collapses to one of the possible states. But here's the key point: this collapse is not just a local event; it's a non-local effect that affects the other particle as well. This is because the entangled wave function is a shared resource that describes the correlation between the two particles.\n\nThe mathematical framework that describes this phenomenon is based on the concept of a \"reduction of the wave function.\" When a measurement is made on a particle, the wave function of that particle is reduced to one of the possible states, but the wave function of the other particle is also affected in a way that's described by the Schrödinger equation.\n\nThe Schrödinger equation is a partial differential equation that describes how the wave function evolves over time.",
